  mix/ship prices the mixer schedule on its own - what the hand-chosen
  ramp gives up. both/drift says whether optimising the mixer adds
  anything ONCE the drift is already optimal, which is the question that
  decides whether every mixer result this session was measured on an
  arbitrary schedule or on a near-optimal one.

================================================================================
READING, for the record:

  mix/ship = 1.85 at m=1.  Optimising beta_s ALONE, with the shipped
  energy-truncation drift untouched, is worth 85%. v7_mixer's suspicion -
  "more likely the uniform beta is simply mistuned and any perturbation of
  the AVERAGE mixing amount helps" - was correct and MATERIAL. v7 could not
  close it because the effect equalled cross-run reproducibility; on the
  deterministic model there is no reproducibility to fight.

  both/drift = 1.19.  The mixer still adds 19% AFTER the drift is optimal,
  so drift and mixer INTERACT and cannot be tuned one at a time. That is the
  methodology used throughout this session.

  opt both / shipped = 2.83 at m=1 (7.207 vs 2.545).

  CONSEQUENCE FOR v39. The global-reflection test compared two mixer FAMILIES
  on the shipped ramp, now known to be 85% off its own optimum. "The Grover
  diffuser changes nothing" was measured on a schedule that suited neither
  family, and should be treated as unsafe rather than as a closed verdict.
